WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.810
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:09.300
In the last few years, neural network-based
learning algorithms became so good at image

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:15.610
recognition tasks that they can often rival,
and sometimes even outperform humans in these

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:16.610
endeavors.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:20.840
Beyond making these neural networks even more
accurate in these tasks, interestingly, there

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:26.070
is also plenty of research work on how to
attack and mislead these neural networks.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:32.250
I think this area of research is extremely
exciting and I’ll now try to show you why.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.840
One of the first examples of an adversarial
attack can be performed as follows.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:42.130
We present such a classifier with an image
of a bus, and it will successfully tell us

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:45.070
that yes, this is indeed a bus.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:46.680
Nothing too crazy here.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:53.000
Now, we show it not an image of a bus, but
a bus plus some carefully crafted noise that

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.310
is barely perceptible, that forces the neural
network to misclassify it as an ostrich.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:03.700
I will stress that this is not any kind of
noise, but the kind of noise that exploits

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:09.670
biases in the neural network, which is, by
no means easy or trivial to craft.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:14.720
However, if we succeed at that, this kind
of adversarial attack can be pulled off on

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.799
many different kinds of images.

00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:21.130
Everything that you see here on the right
will be classified as an ostrich by the neural

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:24.159
network these noise patterns were crafted
for.

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:28.999
In a later work, researchers of the Google
Brain team found that we can not only coerce

00:01:28.999 --> 00:01:34.580
the neural network into making some mistake,
but we can even force it to make exactly the

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.929
kind of mistake we want!

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:43.229
This example here reprograms an image classifier
to count the number of squares in our images.

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:49.310
However, interestingly, some adversarial attacks
do not need carefully crafted noise, or any

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.119
tricks for that matter.

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:55.619
Did you know that many of them occur naturally
in nature!

00:01:55.619 --> 00:02:01.670
This new work contains a brutally hard dataset
with such images that throw off even the best

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:03.720
neural image recognition systems.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.970
Let’s have a look at an example.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:10.750
If I were the neural network, I would look
at this squirrel and claim that “with high

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:15.220
confidence, I can tell you that this is a
sea lion”.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:19.370
And you human, may think that this is a dragonfly,
but you would be wrong.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:23.580
I am pretty sure that this is a manhole cover!

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.080
Except that it’s not.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.660
The paper shows many of these examples, some
of which don’t really occur in my brain.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:35.450
For instance, I don’t see this mushroom
as a pretzel at all, but there was something

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:42.270
about that dragonfly that, upon a cursory
look, may get registered as a manhole cover.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:47.070
If you look quickly, you see a squirrel here…just
kidding, it’s a bullfrog.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:52.140
I feel that if I look at some of these with
a fresh eye, sometimes I get a similar impression

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:53.270
as the neural network.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:57.610
I’ll put up a bunch of more examples for
you here, let me know in the comments which

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.590
are the ones that got you.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:01.910
Very cool project, I love it.

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:07.560
What’s even better, this dataset by the
name ImageNet-A is now available for everyone,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.090
free of charge.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:13.990
And if you remember, at the start of the video,
I said that it is brutally hard for neural

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.870
networks to identify what is going on here.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:20.520
So what kind of success rates can we expect?

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:21.770
70%?

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:23.410
Maybe 50%?

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:24.670
Nope.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:25.940
2%.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.200
Wow.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.540
In a world where some of these learning-based
image classifiers are better than us at some

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:37.940
datasets, they are vastly outclassed by us
humans on these natural adversarial examples.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:41.340
If you have a look at the paper, you will
see that the currently known techniques to

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.430
improve the robustness of training show little
to no improvement to this.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:50.570
I cannot wait to see some followup papers
on how to crack this nut.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:55.300
We can learn so much from this paper, and
will likely learn even more from these followup

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.300
works.

